<details class="how"><summary>How it works</summary>
<h4>Packets, not streams</h4>
<p>Anything you send over the internet is chopped into small pieces called <b>packets</b>. Each one carries a bit of the data plus a label saying where it came from and where it’s going. The little yellow box on the map is one packet.</p>
<h4>Routers pass it along</h4>
<p>A router doesn’t know the whole route. It reads the destination on the label and hands the packet to whichever neighbour is the best next step. That neighbour does the same, and so on until the packet arrives. Each handover is called a <b>hop</b>. This map picks the quickest route by adding up the milliseconds on each link. Real routers work it out by talking to their neighbours, using protocols such as OSPF inside one network and BGP between networks.</p>
<h4>When things break</h4>
<p>If a router fails or a cable is cut, the neighbours notice and send traffic another way. That’s why the internet keeps working when a cable breaks. Packets that were on the broken part at that moment are lost. Most web traffic uses <b>TCP</b>, which notices missing pieces and sends them again. Try it: switch off a router, send a packet, and watch the detour. Break too much and there’s no route left at all.</p>
<h4>Chaos mode</h4>
<p>Chaos mode keeps breaking and repairing random routers and links while it sends packets, so you can watch the network cope. Real companies do the same on purpose to find weak spots before customers do. It’s called chaos engineering.</p>
<h4>The counters</h4>
<p><b>Delivered</b> counts packets that arrived. <b>Lost</b> counts packets that were on the wire when something broke, plus packets that had no route at all.</p>
</details>

<section>
<h3 class="big">A tiny glossary</h3>
<dl>
<div><dt>Packet</dt><dd>A small chunk of data. Big things like photos are split into lots of packets, sent separately and put back together at the end.</dd></div>
<div><dt>IP address</dt><dd>A number that says where a device is, like 192.168.1.20 on a home network. Every device online has one.</dd></div>
<div><dt>DNS</dt><dd>The internet’s phone book. It turns a name like example.com into an IP address your device can use.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Router</dt><dd>A box that reads where a packet is going and passes it on to the next router along the way.</dd></div>
<div><dt>NAT</dt><dd>A router trick that lets many devices share one public address. The router keeps a table of who asked for what.</dd></div>
<div><dt>CGNAT</dt><dd>NAT done by your ISP across lots of homes at once. Cheaper for them, but you lose the ability to accept incoming connections.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Nameserver</dt><dd>A server that holds the IP addresses for a set of domain names. It’s the one that finally has the answer in a DNS lookup.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Traceroute</dt><dd>A tool that lists every router between you and a server, with how long each hop took.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Firewall</dt><dd>A gatekeeper that allows or blocks packets using a list of rules, such as which port they’re heading for.</dd></div>
<div><dt>HTTPS</dt><dd>Web traffic scrambled with encryption, so anyone in the middle sees only gibberish.</dd></div>
<div><dt>VPN</dt><dd>An encrypted tunnel to a server somewhere else. Websites see that server’s address instead of yours.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Botnet</dt><dd>A group of hacked devices controlled by one attacker, often used to flood a website with requests.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Hop</dt><dd>One jump from one router to the next. The more hops, the longer the trip usually takes.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Latency</dt><dd>How long data takes to arrive, measured in milliseconds (ms). Lower is better, especially for games.</dd></div>
</dl>
<p class="fact">Packets from the same file can take different routes and arrive out of order. Your device puts them back together.</p>
</section>
<section id="journey">
<h3 class="big" style="max-width:22ch">How your data finds its way</h3>
<p class="intro">Before a website loads, your device needs two answers: where is it, and how do I get there? Names are for people, so the first job is a phone-book lookup called DNS. Then your packets start hopping from router to router.</p>

<h3 class="mid">1. Find the address (DNS)</h3>
<div class="stage">
<div class="bar">
<label>Domain name<input id="d-in" type="text" value="cats.example" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"></label>
<label>Record<select id="d-ty"><option value="A">A (IPv4 address)</option><option value="AAAA">AAAA (IPv6 address)</option><option value="NS">NS (nameservers)</option><option value="MX">MX (mail server)</option></select></label>
<button class="go" id="d-go">Look it up</button>
<button class="alt" id="d-clr">Clear resolver cache</button>
</div>
<canvas id="dv" aria-label="Diagram of a DNS lookup between you, a resolver, a root server, a top-level domain server and a nameserver."></canvas>
<p class="info" id="d-info">Tap any server on the map to see what it does.</p>
<div class="under">
<div><h2>The conversation</h2><ol id="d-log"></ol></div>
<div><h2>Answer</h2><p class="res" id="d-res"></p></div>
</div>
</div>
<p class="note">Type any domain you like. The addresses are made up, but the same name always gets the same one.</p>

<details class="how"><summary>How it works</summary>
<h4>Names for people, numbers for machines</h4>
<p>Routers move packets using IP addresses. People prefer names like example.com. <b>DNS</b>, the Domain Name System, turns names into addresses. It happens before almost every page you load.</p>
<h4>The chain of questions</h4>
<p>Your device asks a <b>resolver</b>, usually run by your ISP or a public service. The resolver doesn’t know the answer, so it asks a <b>root server</b>, which only knows who looks after each ending such as .com or .org. It points to the <b>TLD server</b> for that ending, which points to the domain’s own <b>nameserver</b>. The nameserver holds the real records and gives the answer. Every step only knows the next step. Nobody holds the whole phone book, which keeps DNS spread out and hard to knock over.</p>
<h4>Caching</h4>
<p>The resolver remembers answers for a set time, chosen by the domain’s owner. Look the same name up twice and the second time is answered from memory. Use <b>Clear resolver cache</b> to see the full trip again.</p>
<h4>Record types</h4>
<p><b>A</b> points a name to an IPv4 address. <b>AAAA</b> points it to an IPv6 address. <b>NS</b> lists the nameservers in charge of the domain. <b>MX</b> says where email for the domain should go, with a number for priority. Pick one in the dropdown and look it up.</p>
<h4>In the demo</h4>
<p>The addresses are made up, and the same name always gets the same one. Real lookups usually take a few tens of milliseconds, and many are answered straight from a cache.</p>
</details>

<div class="cx ba"><span class="lb">Back in the day</span><div class="row"><div><h5>Before 1983</h5>One shared file, HOSTS.TXT, listed every computer on the network. People downloaded fresh copies by hand.</div><div class="ar" aria-hidden="true">→</div><div><h5>Then came DNS</h5>The phone book was split up and spread across the world, so nobody had to keep one giant list.</div></div></div>
<h3 class="mid">2. Follow the hops (traceroute)</h3>
<div class="stage">
<div class="bar">
<label>Destination<select id="t-pre"><option value="near">A server in your city</option><option value="far">A server across the country</option><option value="world">A server on the other side of the world</option></select></label>
