<details class="how"><summary>How it works</summary>
<h4>Counting down with TTL</h4>
<p>Every packet has a number called <b>TTL</b> (time to live). Each router subtracts one as the packet passes. If it reaches zero, the router throws the packet away and sends a short message back saying so. Traceroute uses that on purpose. It sends a packet with TTL 1, and the first router replies. Then TTL 2, and the second router replies. And so on until the packet reaches the server. Each reply reveals who that router is and how long the trip took.</p>
<h4>Reading the list</h4>
<p>The times are round trips: there and back. Normally they grow a little at each hop. A big jump usually means distance or a slow link. Light in glass fibre travels about 200 km every millisecond, so each 1,000 km adds about 5 ms each way. That’s why the world trip jumps when the cable crosses the sea.</p>
<h4>Stars</h4>
<p>A line of stars means a router didn’t reply. Many are set to ignore these probes. Your data still passes through them fine.</p>
<h4>Try it</h4>
<p>Run all three destinations and compare the number of hops and the total time. Tap any hop on the map to see what kind of router it is.</p>
</details>
<div class="cx stat"><div class="n">95%+</div><p>of international internet traffic travels through <b>cables on the sea floor</b>, not through satellites.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="nat">

<h3 class="big" style="max-width:22ch">Why your devices have fake addresses</h3>
<p class="intro">There are only about 4.3 billion IPv4 addresses, and far more devices than that. So your home shares one public address, and every device inside gets a private one like 192.168.1.10. Your router does the swapping. That trick is called NAT, short for network address translation.</p>
<p class="intro">Some ISPs have run out of addresses too. They put another NAT, called CGNAT (carrier-grade NAT), in front of whole neighbourhoods. Now your router doesn’t even get a public address. It gets a shared one starting with 100.64, and many homes use the same public address at once. Try both below.</p>

