<details class="how"><summary>How it works</summary>
<h4>Why NAT exists</h4>
<p>An IPv4 address is 32 bits long, which gives about 4.3 billion addresses. That’s fewer than the number of devices in the world. So some ranges, such as 192.168.x.x, 10.x.x.x and 172.16 to 172.31, are set aside as <b>private</b>. Anyone can reuse them inside their own network because they never appear on the public internet.</p>
<h4>What the router does</h4>
<p>When your laptop sends a request, it uses a private address and a port number. Your router swaps that for its own public address and a fresh port, and writes the pair into a table. When the reply comes back to that public address and port, the router looks it up, swaps back, and hands it to the right device. The ports are what keep many devices apart behind one address.</p>
<h4>Why connections from outside fail</h4>
<p>A packet that arrives out of the blue has no entry in the table. The router has no idea which device it’s meant for, so it drops it. <b>Port forwarding</b> fixes that by adding an entry by hand: anything arriving on port 25565, say, goes to your laptop. That’s how people host game servers at home.</p>
<h4>CGNAT</h4>
<p>Some ISPs don’t have enough public addresses for every customer. So they run a big NAT of their own, called <b>carrier-grade NAT</b>. Your router gets an address from the 100.64.0.0/10 range, which is reserved for exactly this, and the ISP’s box swaps it for a public address shared by many homes. Now there are two tables in a row. You can set a port-forwarding rule on your router, but the ISP’s box has no such rule, so it drops the packet before it ever reaches you.</p>
<h4>Things to try</h4>
<p>Send a request out in each mode and watch the tables fill in. Press <b>Friend connects in</b> with port forward off, then on. Then switch to CGNAT and try it again. In CGNAT mode, press <b>Neighbour sends request</b> to see other homes using the same public address with different ports.</p>
</details>

<div class="cx two"><div class="hd"><span class="lb">Same address, no clash</span></div><div class="cols"><div class="c1"><h5>Your laptop</h5><b>192.168.1.10</b><p>Inside your home.</p></div><div class="c2"><h5>A stranger’s laptop</h5><b>192.168.1.10</b><p>Inside theirs. Same number, and nobody minds, because the two networks never meet on the internet.</p></div></div></div>
<h3 class="big" style="margin-top:96px;max-width:22ch">Why CGNAT is such a pain</h3>
<dl>
<div><dt>You can’t host anything</dt><dd>Game servers, cameras and remote desktop all need someone outside to reach in. Behind CGNAT there’s no way in, because the ISP’s box drops anything nobody asked for.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Port forwarding stops working</dt><dd>You can set the rule on your router perfectly and it still won’t help. The packet is dropped one step earlier, at a box you don’t control.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Peer-to-peer gets slower</dt><dd>Voice calls and games often can’t connect directly, so they send everything through a relay server instead. That adds lag.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Strangers share your address</dt><dd>Many homes can look like one IP address, so a ban or block aimed at one person can hit everyone using it.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Consoles complain</dt><dd>Stack another NAT on top of yours and consoles report a strict NAT type, which limits who you can play with.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Problems are hard to trace</dt><dd>Tools and logs show the shared address, not you, so working out what went wrong often needs your ISP’s help.</dd></div>
</dl>
<div class="ways"><h4>Ways around it</h4><p>Ask your ISP for a public IPv4 address (some charge a small fee). Use IPv6, which has so many addresses that NAT isn’t needed. Or rent a small server somewhere and tunnel your traffic through it.</p></div>
