<details class="how"><summary>How it works</summary>
<h4>Why plain text is risky</h4>
<p>Your data passes through many devices you don’t control: the café’s router, your internet provider, and others. If it isn’t encrypted, anyone along the way can read it. The “evil router” stands for a rogue Wi-Fi hotspot, a hacked router, or someone on your network tricking your device into sending traffic through them. Sitting in the path and reading or changing traffic is called a <b>man-in-the-middle attack</b>.</p>
<h4>What HTTPS does</h4>
<p>HTTPS is normal web traffic wrapped in <b>TLS</b>. Your browser and the website first agree on a secret key using public-key maths, in a way that doesn’t reveal the key to anyone watching. From then on everything is scrambled with that key. The evil router sees only gibberish, and any tampering is detected.</p>
<h4>Certificates</h4>
<p>The website proves who it is with a <b>certificate</b> signed by an authority your device already trusts. That stops the evil router pretending to be the site. If it tries, your browser shows a warning.</p>
<h4>What HTTPS doesn’t hide</h4>
<p>The evil router can still see which address you’re talking to, and roughly how much data you send. It just can’t read what’s inside.</p>
<h4>In the demo</h4>
<p>Drop the evil router on the path and send a login. The scroll shows what it reads. Then switch HTTPS on: the packet turns into a padlock and the scroll shows scrambled symbols. The gibberish here is random characters. Real encrypted data looks like random bytes.</p>
</details>
<div class="ways"><h4>How to stay safe</h4><p>Look for https:// in the address bar before you type a password. Be careful on public Wi-Fi, and use your phone’s data or a VPN if you can’t trust the network. Keep your devices updated, because updates close the holes attackers use.</p></div>

<div class="cx two"><div class="hd"><span class="lb">What HTTPS hides</span></div><div class="cols"><div class="c2"><h5>🔒 Hidden</h5><p>Your password, the page you’re on, everything you type and read.</p></div><div class="c1"><h5>👀 Still visible</h5><p>Which site you’re talking to, and roughly how much data you send.</p></div></div></div>
<div class="toyhead"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">🌍</span><div><h3>Teleport to the UK</h3><p>Your request gets turned away at the border. A tunnel gets you through.</p></div></div>
<div class="stage">
<div class="bar">
