<details class="how"><summary>How it works</summary>
<h4>The problem</h4>
<p>BBC iPlayer is made for people in the United Kingdom. When you open it, the BBC’s servers look at the <b>IP address</b> your request came from and check it against a database that says which country each address belongs to. An address from outside the UK gets turned away.</p>
<h4>What a VPN does</h4>
<p>A VPN (virtual private network) is an app that builds an <b>encrypted tunnel</b> from your device to a server run by the VPN company. Pick a server in the UK and your request goes into the tunnel, comes out at that server, and is sent on to iPlayer from the server’s own UK address. iPlayer sees the VPN server, not you. The video then comes back the same way: to the VPN server, down the tunnel, and to your screen.</p>
<h4>The second benefit: privacy on the way</h4>
<p>Between you and the VPN server everything is encrypted. Someone on the café Wi-Fi, or your internet provider, can see that you’re talking to a VPN but not which sites you visit. The catch is that the VPN company can now see that instead, so you’re moving trust from one company to another, not removing it.</p>
<h4>What a VPN doesn’t do</h4>
<p>It doesn’t make you anonymous. Logging in, cookies and your browser can still identify you. Streaming services also try to spot and block the addresses of known VPN servers, so it often doesn’t work. Some services forbid this in their terms, so check before you try it for real.</p>
<h4>In the demo</h4>
<p>The purple area is your country and the green area is the UK. The glowing pipe is the encrypted tunnel. Try it with the VPN off, then on, and compare the two lists on the right.</p>
</details>
<h4 class="sub">What a VPN is good for</h4>
<dl><div><dt>Café and hotel Wi-Fi</dt><dd>Everything between you and the VPN server is scrambled, so other people on the same network can’t read it.</dd></div><div><dt>Hiding from your provider</dt><dd>Your internet provider can see that you’re using a VPN, but not which sites you visit.</dd></div><div><dt>Changing your location</dt><dd>Websites see the VPN server’s address, so you appear to be wherever it is.</dd></div><div><dt>What it can’t do</dt><dd>It won’t stop a site recognising you once you log in, and it won’t protect you from a bad download.</dd></div></dl>

<div class="cx myth"><span class="lb">Myth</span><p class="q" style="margin:0;color:#fff">“A VPN makes me anonymous.”</p><p><b>Reality:</b> it moves who can see your traffic, from your internet provider to the VPN company. Logins, cookies and your browser can still identify you.</p></div>
<div class="toyhead"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">🛡️</span><div><h3>Be the bouncer</h3><p>Customers on the left, attackers mixed in. Block the right ports before five get past.</p></div></div>
