<div class="under">
<div><h2>Live stats</h2><p class="res" id="dd-out"></p></div>
<div><h2>What to watch</h2><p class="res">The yellow dots are attack requests. The green dots are a real customer’s requests. Each bot here sends about 10 requests a second. The router can handle roughly 60,000 a second and the website roughly 4,000. When the website is full, the customer’s requests get dropped too.</p></div>
</div></div>
<details class="how"><summary>How it works</summary>
<h4>What a botnet is</h4>
<p>A <b>botnet</b> is a large group of hacked devices that one attacker controls remotely. The devices are ordinary things: home computers, Wi-Fi routers, cameras, smart TVs. They usually get infected through weak or default passwords or software that was never updated. Their owners often notice nothing, apart from maybe a slow connection.</p>
<h4>Command and control</h4>
<p>The attacker doesn’t talk to each bot one by one. Every bot checks in with a <b>command server</b>, and the attacker sends one order to it that reaches all of them at once. In the demo, press <b>Send attack command</b> and watch the order travel from the attacker to the command server and out to the bots.</p>
<h4>The flood</h4>
<p>When the order arrives, every bot starts sending requests to the target. One bot sends very little, but thousands together send a huge amount. Every link, router and server has a limit on how much it can handle. Past that limit, requests queue up, then get dropped, and everyone gets slow responses or none at all, including real customers. That’s a <b>DDoS</b>: a distributed denial of service. It’s distributed because the traffic comes from many places and denial because genuine users are denied service.</p>
<h4>Why it’s hard to stop</h4>
<p>The traffic comes from thousands of ordinary-looking devices in many countries, so blocking one address does nothing. The attack requests can also look like normal page visits.</p>
<h4>Defences</h4>
<p>Big providers run <b>scrubbing</b> systems that sit in front of a site, spot attack patterns and throw most of the junk away before it arrives. Content delivery networks spread a site across many data centres so no single one is overwhelmed. Sites can also limit how many requests one address may send. Turn on the <b>Shield</b> to see a filter removing most of the attack before it reaches the website.</p>
<h4>About the numbers</h4>
<p>The slider sets how many bots the attacker has, from 1 to 100,000. The capacities and the 10-requests-a-second figure are made up for the demo. The 48 icons stand for however many bots you choose.</p>
</details>
<h4 class="sub">Why a botnet is so hard to stop</h4>
<dl><div><dt>Too many addresses</dt><dd>Blocking one address does nothing when the traffic comes from thousands.</dd></div><div><dt>It looks normal</dt><dd>Each request can look like an ordinary visit to the page.</dd></div><div><dt>Spread everywhere</dt><dd>Bots sit in many countries, so there’s no single place to switch off.</dd></div><div><dt>Owners don’t notice</dt><dd>A hacked camera still films. Its owner might only see a slightly slower connection.</dd></div></dl>

<div class="cx case"><span class="lb">Case file</span><p><b>Mirai, 2016.</b> A botnet built mostly from cameras and home routers still using their <b>factory passwords</b>. Nobody had hacked anything clever. They just tried the default login.</p></div>
<div class="toyhead"><span class="num" aria-hidden="true">🌐</span><div><h3>Run out of internet</h3><p>Eighteen devices, one public address. See what happens when everyone gets their own.</p></div></div>
<div class="stage">
<div class="bar">
<span style="display:flex;gap:8px"><button class="tog" id="iv-4" aria-pressed="true">IPv4</button><button class="tog" id="iv-6" aria-pressed="false">IPv6</button></span>
</div>
<canvas id="iv" class="lab-c" aria-label="Eighteen devices. In IPv4 they are crowded behind one address. In IPv6 each has its own."></canvas>
<p class="info" id="iv-info"></p>
<div class="under"><div><h2>The numbers</h2><p class="res" id="iv-res"></p></div><div><h2>Tap a device</h2><p class="res" id="iv-dev">Tap any device on the map to see its address.</p></div></div>
</div>

<details class="how"><summary>How it works</summary>
<h4>Running out of addresses</h4>
<p>An IPv4 address is 32 bits, which gives about <b>4.3 billion</b> addresses. That seemed like plenty in the 1980s. Today there are far more devices than that, and the regional registries that hand addresses out have run out of fresh ones. The workaround is NAT, where a whole home shares one public address.</p>
<h4>A much bigger space</h4>
<p>An IPv6 address is 128 bits long, which gives about <b>340 undecillion</b> addresses (3.4 followed by 38 zeros). It’s written in hexadecimal groups, like 2001:db8:1::a0. The range starting 2001:db8 is reserved for examples.</p>
<h4>What changes</h4>
<p>With IPv6 every device can have its own public address. No sharing, no translation table, no port juggling. Video calls, game hosting and similar things work more directly, and routing gets simpler. ISPs that roll out IPv6 also need less CGNAT.</p>
<h4>What doesn’t change</h4>
<p>Having a public address doesn’t mean anyone can connect. A firewall still decides what gets in, and most routers block unrequested incoming traffic by default.</p>
<h4>Both at once</h4>
<p>The internet is running IPv4 and IPv6 side by side right now. Most devices can speak both, and the move is slow because every network along the way has to support it.</p>
</details>
<h4 class="sub">Why isn’t everyone on IPv6 yet?</h4>
<dl><div><dt>Everyone has to move</dt><dd>Every network between you and a website has to handle it, and each moves at its own pace.</dd></div><div><dt>NAT works well enough</dt><dd>Sharing one address is annoying, but it keeps things running, so there’s little pressure to change.</dd></div><div><dt>Old gear</dt><dd>Some older devices and software only understand IPv4.</dd></div><div><dt>Both at once</dt><dd>That’s why most networks run IPv4 and IPv6 side by side for now.</dd></div></dl>


<div class="cx stat"><div class="n">2011</div><p>The year the last big blocks of fresh IPv4 addresses were handed out. Since then, networks have mostly been <b>reusing and sharing</b> what’s left.</p></div>
<h3 class="big" style="margin-top:88px;max-width:22ch">Why does Wi-Fi get worse when it’s crowded?</h3>
<p class="intro">Every device nearby shares one invisible channel. Send at the wrong moment and nobody hears anything. See how devices take turns.</p>
<div class="stage">
<div class="bar">
<button class="go" id="wf-both">Both send at once</button>
<button class="alt" id="wf-one">Only one sends</button>
<button id="wf-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
<canvas id="wv" class="lab-c" aria-label="A phone and a laptop talk to a Wi-Fi router. If they send at the same moment their packets collide."></canvas>
<div class="under">
<div><h2>What happened</h2><ol id="wf-log"><li class="empty">Press a button to start.</li></ol></div>
<div><h2>The rule</h2><p class="res">Radio can only carry one message at a time. When two collide, both devices wait a random number of slots and try again. Each collision doubles the range, so they soon stop clashing. Old wired Ethernet detected collisions (CSMA/CD). Wi-Fi tries to avoid them (CSMA/CA).</p></div>
</div></div>
<details class="how"><summary>How it works</summary>
<h4>One shared channel</h4>
<p>Wi-Fi sends data as radio waves, and every device nearby shares the same channel. If two devices transmit at exactly the same moment, the signals garble each other and the router can’t read either. That’s a <b>collision</b>.</p>
<h4>Listen first</h4>
<p>Wi-Fi devices listen before they talk. If the channel is busy, they wait. After each message the receiver sends a short acknowledgement. No acknowledgement means something went wrong, so the sender tries again. This approach is called <b>CSMA/CA</b>, carrier sense multiple access with collision avoidance.</p>
<h4>Random backoff</h4>
<p>After a collision, each device picks a random waiting time measured in short slots. The first time the choice is 0 or 1 slots. After the next collision it’s 0 to 3, then 0 to 7, and so on. The range doubles each time, so devices soon stop choosing the same slot. If they do pick the same one, they collide again, and that’s what the demo shows. A device that’s waiting for its slot but hears the other one talking keeps listening until the channel is free.</p>
<h4>And Ethernet?</h4>
<p>Old Ethernet on a shared cable could detect a collision while sending, then stop and back off. That’s <b>CSMA/CD</b>, collision detection. Modern wired networks use switches and separate send and receive wires, so collisions basically don’t happen any more.</p>
<h4>In the demo</h4>
<p>Press <b>Both send at once</b> and watch the collision, the random wait slots and the retry. Run it a few times. Sometimes they pick the same slot again.</p>
</details>
<div class="ways"><h4>Things that help</h4><p>Use the 5 GHz band if your router has one, since fewer neighbours crowd it. Plug fixed devices like consoles and TVs in with a cable so they stop using the air. And try a quieter channel in your router’s settings.</p></div>

<div class="cx eq"><span class="lb">Why the Wi-Fi drops when you reheat lunch</span><div class="row"><div class="tk">📶 Wi-Fi<small>2.4 GHz band</small></div><div class="op">≈</div><div class="tk">🍲 Microwave<small>about 2.45 GHz</small></div><div class="op">=</div><div class="tk bad">💥 Interference</div></div><p>They use almost the same radio frequency, so a microwave oven can leak noise that drowns out nearby Wi-Fi.</p></div>
